    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Hire a public adjuster when the claim is large or disputed enough that better documentation and negotiation will likely recover more than the fee, which Texas caps at 10% of the settlement. If the damage is modest, coverage is not in question, and the insurer's estimate already matches what a reputable contractor would charge, you probably do not need one. Handle it yourself and keep the whole check.</p>
<p>That is the honest version. The rest of this page is a way to tell which situation you are in.</p>

<h2>When you probably don't need a public adjuster</h2>
<p>If your claim fits most of this list, you can likely finish it on your own, and in a free review we would tell you so.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li><strong>The damage is small.</strong> A few missing shingles, one stained ceiling, a single broken window. If the total repair is not much above your deductible, a percentage fee can cost you more than any improvement in the offer.</li>
<li><strong>Coverage is obvious.</strong> A pipe burst, the plumber's invoice names the cause, the water was cleaned up fast and the policy clearly covers sudden water damage.</li>
<li><strong>The first offer is fair.</strong> You got a contractor's estimate, compared it to the insurer's line by line, and they are close. Small differences can often be settled with a phone call and a photo.</li>
<li><strong>You have the time and the patience.</strong> You can read your policy, keep a claim log, follow up in writing and push back on a missed item. Plenty of people do this well.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before deciding the offer is fair, get the insurer's estimate in writing and hand it to your contractor. Ask them to mark every line that is missing or priced too low. That one exercise answers most of this question.</p></div>

<h2>When a public adjuster is usually worth a call</h2>
<p>These are the situations where representation tends to change the result, because the money is in details the first inspection is likely to miss.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The offer is far below your contractor's estimate.</strong> A gap of thousands of dollars almost always comes down to specific line items, measurements or code items. That is exactly what a public adjuster is trained to document. See <a href="/post/underpaid-hail-damage-insurance-claim/">underpaid hail damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Part of the claim was denied.</strong> "Wear and tear," "pre-existing," "cosmetic only," or "long-term leak" can each be right or wrong, depending on the evidence. Our <a href="/services/denied-underpaid-claims/">denied and underpaid claims</a> service is built around these disputes.</li>
<li><strong>The loss is large or spans several trades.</strong> A fire, a hurricane, or a water loss that reaches flooring, cabinets, drywall, insulation and contents involves hundreds of line items plus additional living expenses. Missing one category can be a five-figure mistake.</li>
<li><strong>It is a commercial property.</strong> Business income, code upgrades, tenant improvements and multiple buildings make commercial claims harder to put together. See <a href="/commercial-claims/">commercial claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>The policy has traps.</strong> A percentage wind and hail deductible, an ACV roof schedule, a cosmetic damage exclusion or a matching dispute can each change the payout more than the damage itself.</li>
<li><strong>The claim has stalled.</strong> Texas gives insurers set deadlines to acknowledge, decide and pay (Chapter 542 of the Insurance Code). If those dates are slipping and your emails go unanswered, someone needs to put the claim back on track. Our <a href="/tools/texas-claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> shows where yours should be.</li>
<li><strong>You don't have the hours.</strong> A serious claim can take months of inspections, re-inspections, documents and calls. If you are working, caring for family and living in a hotel, that time has a real cost.</li>
</ul>

[[tool:do-i-need-a-public-adjuster]]

<h2>A simple test: does the fee pay for itself?</h2>
<p>The fee comes out of the claim payment, so the question is whether the claim will grow by more than the fee. These are hypothetical round numbers to show the logic, not results from real claims.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div><h3>Probably not worth it</h3><ul>
<li>Insurer offers $9,000.</li>
<li>Your roofer's estimate is $10,000.</li>
<li>Even if a public adjuster closes the whole gap, a 10% fee on $10,000 is $1,000.</li>
<li>You would net about the same as accepting the offer, after more time and effort.</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Probably worth a review</h3><ul>
<li>Insurer offers $9,000.</li>
<li>Your roofer's estimate is $28,000, and interior damage was never inspected.</li>
<li>If documentation supports a $28,000 claim, a 10% fee is $2,800.</li>
<li>You would net about $25,200 against the $9,000 offer.</li>
</ul></div>
</div>
<p>Nobody can promise the second outcome; it depends on the damage, the policy and the evidence. But the shape of the decision is clear: the bigger and better-supported the gap, the more sense representation makes. For how the fee is calculated on real payments, including partial payments and recoverable depreciation, see <a href="/post/the-truth-about-public-adjuster-fees/">public adjuster fees in Texas</a>, or plug your own numbers into the <a href="/tools/claim-settlement-calculator/">claim settlement calculator</a>.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>The honest downsides</h2>
<p>A public adjuster is not free, and it is not the right tool for every problem.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>You share the payment.</strong> Up to 10% of what the insurer pays goes to the adjuster. On a claim that was already fairly priced, that is money you did not need to spend.</li>
<li><strong>Not every public adjuster is good.</strong> Licensing sets a floor, not a ceiling. Check the license, read the contract and ask hard questions. Our <a href="/post/smart-property-owners-guide-to-choosing-the-right-public-adjuster/">guide to choosing a public adjuster</a> lists what to ask.</li>
<li><strong>Some disputes need a lawyer instead.</strong> If the insurer has denied coverage outright, says you breached the policy, or a lawsuit is on the table, an attorney is the right first call. Public adjusters work the facts and the amount; attorneys work the legal questions. We work alongside attorneys when a claim needs both.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Other routes worth knowing</h2>
<p>A public adjuster is one option among several. Depending on the problem:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Handle it yourself.</strong> See the steps below.</li>
<li><strong>Appraisal.</strong> If you and the insurer agree the loss is covered but disagree on the amount, many policies let either side demand appraisal. Each side picks an appraiser, and the two pick an umpire. See <a href="/post/appraisal-for-hail-damage-claim/">appraisal for hail damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>A TDI complaint.</strong> If the insurer is ignoring you or missing deadlines, the Texas Department of Insurance accepts consumer complaints.</li>
<li><strong>An attorney.</strong> For coverage denials, bad-faith conduct or a lawsuit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>If you decide to handle it yourself</h2>
<p>Plenty of Texas homeowners finish their own claims well. If that is your choice, work it like a public adjuster would:</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Get the insurer's estimate itemized.</strong> Ask for the full line-item estimate, not just a payment summary. You cannot dispute a total.</li>
<li><strong>Get your own estimate.</strong> A reputable contractor's itemized estimate gives you something to compare against, line by line.</li>
<li><strong>Put every disagreement in writing.</strong> List each missing or underpriced item with a photo and, where you can, the policy language that covers it. Ask for a re-inspection if areas were skipped.</li>
<li><strong>Track the insurer's deadlines.</strong> Note when you sent each requested document; Chapter 542 timelines run from there.</li>
<li><strong>Know your next step.</strong> If the gap stays large, you can still bring in a public adjuster, demand appraisal, or talk to an attorney. Our <a href="/post/common-property-insurance-claims-mistakes/">common claim mistakes</a> post covers the traps to avoid meanwhile.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What a free review should tell you</h2>
<p>A legitimate public adjuster should be willing to tell you when you don't need them. When we review a claim, we look at the policy, the insurer's estimate and the damage, and give you a straight answer: what we think was missed, roughly what it is worth, and whether our fee makes sense against that. If the answer is that you should take the offer, we say so. If you are still unsure where your claim stands, <a href="/post/when-is-it-too-late-to-hire-a-public-adjuster/">when is it too late to hire a public adjuster</a> explains what can still be done at each stage.</p>
